    fact: "In Evelyn Waugh's Brideshead Revisited (1945), Sebastian's slide from radiant Oxford undergraduate to chronic alcoholic is the spine of the novel; his decline runs in deliberate parallel to his family's Catholic faith, the drink standing in for a grace he cannot bring himself to accept. He ends not dead but diminished — drifting through North Africa and finally attaching himself to a monastery in Tunisia as a kind of beloved holy hanger-on, still lapsing into drink, tended and forgiven by the monks. Aloysius, the teddy bear he carries through his Oxford years, was modelled on Archibald Ormsby-Gore, the real childhood bear of Waugh's friend the poet John Betjeman, who brought it up to Oxford with him in the 1920s.",
