    fact: "Caravaggio's surviving police records and trial transcripts run to many pages, among them a 1604 suit brought by a tavern waiter who claimed the painter had thrown a plate of artichokes in his face after a dispute over whether they had been cooked in oil or butter. In late May 1606 a fight reportedly sparked by a wager on a pallacorda (real tennis) match ended with Caravaggio killing Ranuccio Tomassoni; he was sentenced to beheading and an open bounty was decreed, sending him into permanent exile. He died at Porto Ercole, in Tuscany, on 18 July 1610, aged thirty-eight; a 2018 study argued the cause was sepsis from a wound taken in an earlier Naples brawl, though contemporaries variously blamed fever, lead, or malaria.",
